    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Champa was less a single kingdom than a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> principalities along the coast, Indrapura, Amaravati, Vijaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauthara">Kauthara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a>, united by language and culture, with a king of kings in some periods.</li><li>Between the 7th and 10th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> fleets controlled the spice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, and eaglewood from the highlands was the kingdom's chief export.</li><li>The Vietnamese conquest of Vijaya in 1471, in which 120,000 people were killed or captured, reduced Champa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauthara">Kauthara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a>; Panduranga was annexed by Minh Mạng in 1832.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Đông Yên Châu inscription, found in Champa and dated to around 350 CE, is the oldest known text written in a native Southeast Asian language, centuries before the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, Mon or Malay texts.</p></aside>
    </section>

