    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/my-son"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/my-son.jpg" alt="Mỹ Sơn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/my-son">Mỹ Sơn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:X%C3%A3_M%E1%BB%B9_S%C6%A1n,_Ninh_S%C6%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nguyenthuanduc, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Champa grew out of the Sa Huỳnh culture, an Austronesian seafaring people settled on the coast of present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> between about 1000 BCE and 200 CE, who traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> with the Philippine archipelago and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> language belongs to the Austronesian family and is most closely related to Acehnese in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. The first state in the region was Lâm Ấp, known to the Chinese as Linyi, founded in 192 when Khu Liên (Ou Lian), an official of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> at Xianglin, rebelled against Chinese rule; how Linyi related to the later Champa is not clear.</p><p>From about the 4th century the Cham polities absorbed Indian influences, probably through wars with the neighbouring Kingdom of Funan and the acquisition of Funanese territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> became the scholarly language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> the state religion, and red brick <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples rose along the coast. The first king named in the inscriptions is Bhadravarman (r. 380–413), who at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-son">Mỹ Sơn</a> established a linga called Bhadresvara, joining his own name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>'s; the god-king was worshipped there for centuries afterwards. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangaraja">Gangaraja</a> (r. 413–?) abdicated and went on pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, perhaps the only Southeast Asian ruler known to have travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Rudravarman I (r. 529–572) descended from Gangaraja through the maternal line, and the 4th-century Vo Canh inscription already records matrilineal descent among the early Cham rulers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/incense"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/incense.jpg" alt="Incense" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/incense">Incense</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incense_burner_at_Yakuo-in.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nakimusi, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Unlike most mainland Southeast Asian kingdoms, Champa did not rest on agriculture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, a seafaring people, built a trading network from the ports of Hội An and Thị Nại into the mountainous hinterland, with a chain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> along the coast and on the islands of Cù Lao Chàm and Lý Sơn to water Cham and foreign ships. Most exports came from the highlands, from as far as Attapeu in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>: gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, slaves, animals and animal products, and precious woods. The most important was eaglewood, the one product <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> mentioned; in 1155 a Cham envoy shipped 55,020 catties, about 33 tons, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court. The kilns of Gò Sành near Vijaya made olive-green and brown glazed pottery, at its peak in the 14th to 16th centuries and found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the Emirates, Malaysia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><p>Wealth was concentrated in port-cities: Simhapura, a riverine port and political centre from about 400 CE, Amarendrapura and Visnupura in the north, Indrapura, Amaravati and Vijaya in the centre, Nha Trang, Virapura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a> in the south, crowded with Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, Malay, Viet, Arab and Indian traders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-fan-zhi">Zhu Fan Zhi</a> of about 1225 says officials boarded each arriving ship with a leather-bound register, took 20 per cent of the cargo as tax and confiscated the whole if anything was hidden. Kenneth R. Hall holds that Champa lived by raiding rather than by taxes on trade; Hardy argues that its prosperity rested above all on commerce.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii.jpg" alt="Jayavarman VII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JayavarmanVII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Suzan Black, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Compared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Đại Việt, Champa's army was small and loosely organised. Its strength was at sea: between the 7th and 10th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> fleets controlled the spice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and added piracy and raiding to their income. The port-cities were walled; Khu Túc on the Kiến Giang had a revetted wall and moat by the 4th century, and when the Chinese sacked it in 446 every inhabitant over fifteen was put to the sword and some 48,000 of gold carried off.</p><p>Champa was attacked from every side: by the Chinese in 605, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> sacked Trà Kiệu; by Javanese or Kunlun raiders who demolished the temples of Po Nagar in 774 and Virapura in 787 and held the southern coast until Indravarman I drove them off in 799; by the Khmer, who conquered northern Champa in 1145 and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> in the 1190s made it a dependency for thirty years; by the Vietnamese in 982, 1044, 1069, 1073, 1446 and 1471; and by the Mongol Yuan in 1283–85. The Cham struck back. In 803 they seized Nghệ An from the Tang, and in June 1177 Jaya Indravarman IV's surprise naval assault plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, killed the Khmer king and began a four-year occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Che Bong Nga (r. 1360–1390) launched six invasions of Đại Việt, sacking its capital in 1371, 1377, 1378 and 1383, until he was killed in a naval battle in 1390 in which the Vietnamese used firearms for the first time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/panduranga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panduranga.jpg" alt="Panduranga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syayambhuvithoba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Balkrishna Kulkarni., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruler bore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> titles, raja-di-raja, king of kings, or pu po tana raya, lord of all territories, and regnal names formed from prefixes such as Jaya, Maha and Sri with the -varman suffix of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> class. He was the patron of the great temples, where kings erected stone lingas named after themselves with the ending -iśvara, and he was consecrated in the abhiseka ceremony. From the 17th century, after the royal family converted to Bani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, kings also used the Malay title Paduka Seri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>.</p><p>How far Champa was ever one state is disputed. Georges Maspero and George Coedès wrote of a unified kingdom; Po <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> and Trần Quốc Vượng, from the 1980s, read the Chinese texts, the inscriptions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> annals, the Sakkarai dak rai patao, as evidence of several Champas, a confederation of kingdoms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> in which, for periods between the 700s and 1471, a king of kings at Indrapura or Vijaya held sway over lesser kings and regional commanders within a mandala. Under Prakasadharma (r. 653–686) the realm stretched from Quảng Bình to Khánh Hòa and was divided into districts called viṣaya; in the 12th and 13th centuries northern Champa comprised Amaravati, Ulik, Vvyar, Jriy and Traik, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a> stayed quietly autonomous, kept its own list of kings from the 13th century to 1832 and conducted its own foreign affairs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="Kublai Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Araniko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Champa was a link in the spice trade from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and, later, a supplier of aloe on the Arab routes. Chinese histories of the 7th century counted it the chief tributary state of the South, on a par with Koguryŏ and Baekje, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> ordered a mobile secretariat set up there to dominate the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> merchants settled in Sulu from the 10th to the 13th centuries, where they were called Orang Dampuan; Champa traded with Ma-i and Butuan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. Cham princesses married Javanese kings: Princess Daravati is said to have led her husband Kertawijaya and the Majapahit royal family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>The Vietnamese who absorbed Champa were changed by it. In 1044 Lý Thái Tông brought back some 5,000 prisoners, among them court dancers, and in 1060 ordered his musicians to study Cham drum rhythms and songs he had translated himself; bricks inscribed in Cham script show that many Vietnamese temples were built by Cham engineers. Cham culture shaped the Acehnese language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and the Tsat speakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>. Denys Lombard called Champa "not only the name of a former kingdom but it is also of a vast network that extended all over the main Southeast Asian centers". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-son">Mỹ Sơn</a> and Hội An are World Heritage Sites, and the Bacam are among the few surviving non-Indic indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> peoples in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kauthara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kauthara.jpg" alt="Kauthara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kauthara">Kauthara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PonNagarChamTowers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wileypics, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Signs of decline appear long before the end: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions stopped in 1253, no grand temple was built after 1300, and between 1307 and 1401 no indigenous source survives at all. No inscription survives after 1456. In early 1471 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a>'s army destroyed Vijaya and most of northern Champa: 120,000 people were captured or killed, 20–30,000 deported along with fifty members of the royal family, and King Tra Toan died on the way north in captivity. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> envoy told the Chinese court that "Annam destroyed our country". Champa shrank to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauthara">Kauthara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduranga">Panduranga</a>; Kauthara was annexed in 1653, and from 1799 Panduranga's kings were appointed by the court at Huế.</p><p>In August 1832 Minh Mạng, purging the faction of Lê Văn Duyệt, accused the Cham leaders of supporting him, had the last king Po Phaok The and the viceroy Po Dhar Kaok arrested, and made Panduranga the provinces of Ninh Thuận and Bình Thuận under Vietnamese bureaucrats. Cham customary law was abolished, Vietnamese speech and customs made compulsory, settlers seized most Cham farmland, and heavy taxes and conscription followed. Revolts in 1833–1835, the second led by the Bani cleric Ja Thak Wa, briefly refounded a Cham state before Minh Mạng crushed them; in the massacres of 1832–1836 Bani mosques were razed, temples burned and the Cham lost their seafaring and shipbuilding traditions. Only about 40,000 Cham remained in the old Panduranga when the French completed their conquest in 1885.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Champa was a confederation of Austronesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> principalities on the coast of present-day central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and later partly Muslim, which from Lâm Ấp's revolt against the Han in 192 built a maritime trading power at its height in the 9th and 10th centuries, lost Vijaya to Đại Việt in 1471 and was annexed by Minh Mạng in 1832.</em></p>

